From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:50:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: a window kept out of view at a top corner is not asked
 again at every change of displays

Asked through Accessibility for y 0 and for y -50 where the visible frame begins at 31, a window went to y 31 and kept its x and its size:
eight times out of eight, one AppKit test window on one hosted Mac, with the Dock at the bottom, at the left and hidden, and beside a
second display. All eight stand in the middle of a display. The stand-in stopped a window at the bottom only; `whereMacOSPuts` now
stops it at the top as well.

Arbor's own reckoning knew the bottom only, and two of its parking places, the display's top corners, lie under the menu bar. No Mac has
been watched at a corner; by the eight calls:

- A window sent there would come to rest below the menu bar, which Arbor takes for a refusal: at every report of the displays it asks
  again. `isAsFarAsItGoes` knows the stop now. It is right whichever way macOS answers.
- The strip that the menu bar pushes into view was not counted when the corners are weighed against each other. `whereItStops` counts it.
- A window in view that lies above its display's visible top is no longer asked for its size where it stands, where the question would make
  it hop. It goes straight to its slot.

Which display stops a corner that lies on none is a guess, the nearest, which the stand-in shares. At the top it decided nothing before. Now
it can decide where a window waits, in staggered arrangements of three displays: `whereTheGuessDecides` pins one and says so. In
every ordinary arrangement the choice is as it was.

Nothing is learnt of a window's title bar from the stop at the top, as before. `isInAParkingCorner` still knows the bottom only.
